# .graphifyignore — what the CODE GRAPH must not index. Copy into a repo root and edit.
#
# WHY: graphify indexes the DISK, not git. Untracked local directories — worktrees, vendored
# dist bundles, scratch dirs, data drops — become graph NODES. Measured on one fleet in
# 2026-08: three heavily-used repos had agent-facing graphs that were MAJORITY scratch, the
# worst at 76% of 99,877 nodes. In that repo `main()` resolved to NINE different source
# roots, so asking the graph where a symbol lived returned several answers from several
# copies of the same code with no signal which one ships.
#
# That is worse than a stale graph. Stale is old truth; this is confident fiction. And
# recency HIDES it — a graph rebuilt an hour ago can still be 76% scratch.
#
# CRITICAL: graphify PREFERS this file and does NOT also read .gitignore when it exists
# (detect.py: `if not ignore_file.exists()`). So reproduce your .gitignore entries here or
# you will silently start indexing __pycache__, node_modules, and build output.
#
# Find what to add:  python3 ops/graphify/graphify-drift
# It reports the untracked directories contributing nodes, largest first.

# --- reproduce your .gitignore here (graphify will not read it separately) ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
node_modules/
dist/
build/
.venv/
venv/

# --- untracked local scratch: verify each is 0 files tracked before adding ---
# Check with:  git ls-files <dir> | head -1
# A dir with tracked files is REAL SOURCE — excluding it silently deletes code from the
# graph. On one repo `src/` and `static/` looked untracked but held build output, while
# `ac-baseline/` was a full 24MB second copy of the repo. Only the second should be excluded.
#
# my-worktree/
# vendored-dist/
# scratch/
# tmp/
